// @test: typecheck-pass
// @level: L0
// @tier: 0
// @tags: modules, mount, name-resolution, submodule
// @skip: T0795 is open — the bare-path mount of an axiom-only submodule still
//        reports E401. This spec states the expectation and is the acceptance
//        control for that task; it is NOT skipped because the case is
//        uninteresting, and the skip comes off in the commit that fixes the
//        resolution, not by editing the expectation.
//
// Pin (T0795) — `mount a.b.c;` where `a.b.c` names a MODULE must mount the
// module, whatever else has been loaded first.
//
// The meaning of that statement depends on load order today.  The branch
// asks the module registry, which answers only for modules something has
// already loaded — and a bare-path mount is frequently the very statement
// that would have loaded this one.  On a miss it falls through to "treat `c`
// as an item of module `a.b`" and reported
//
//     error<E401>: cannot find `diakrisis` in module `core.math.frameworks`
//
// from inside that module's OWN mod.vr.
//
// Measured over the 68 sites in `core/` where a `mod.vr` re-exports its own
// child by full path: 38 resolved and 30 did not, split exactly by whether
// the child declared a public `fn` — the `fn` being not the mechanism but the
// thing that gets the module loaded by an earlier pass.  Proof in one file,
// measured today:
//
//     mount core.math.frameworks.diakrisis;      E401
//     ---
//     mount core.math.frameworks.diakrisis.*;    ok
//     mount core.math.frameworks.diakrisis;      ok    <- same line, now fine
//
// So the statement's meaning is decided by what preceded it, which is the
// whole defect.
//
// `diakrisis` is chosen because its public surface is axioms only, which is
// the shape that never gets loaded on its own.  A module with a public `fn`
// passes this pin even with the defect fully in place.
//
// That is not hypothetical.  The sibling spec
// `mount_unbraced_archive_submodule.vr` pins this exact mount form and is
// GREEN, because it mounts `core.net.addr` — a module full of
// types and functions, and therefore always already loaded.  A gate over the
// right syntax can still miss the defect entirely if its input is on the easy
// side of the split, so the two specs are complementary rather than
// redundant: that one fixes the form, this one fixes the input.

mount core.math.frameworks.diakrisis;

fn main() {
    print("ok");
}
